+# Decision: Board delivery over self-hosted WebSocket connections
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard must show a committed stall-status change in every connected coordinator's browser within 15 seconds, for up to 800 concurrent browsers across 40 markets, with bursts of 20 committed edits per second at opening time. The server already assigns each committed edit a single authoritative event revision; the question this decision settles is how that revision reaches browsers.
+
+Three delivery routes can carry a committed revision to a browser, and all three meet the freshness goal: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSocket connections, and a hosted realtime service. The choice is not about whether the board updates in time — it is about what the board's day-to-day cost and operational surface look like once a route is chosen.
+
+Two properties of this domain make the choice worth weighing carefully. The delivery cost scales with the number of concurrent browsers and with the number of published market hours, not with the number of edits: forty markets open on weekends, whether or not anything changes, so the recurring cost of a route is paid across every market session and every idle hour between them. And the data on the wire is operational — stall IDs and statuses — not customer records, so the case for keeping it inside project infrastructure is a matter of coupling and cost rather than of regulatory necessity.
+
+The stake of getting this wrong is asymmetric in both directions. A route that turns out too slow to use pushes the project into per-browser refresh habits that coordinators stop trusting. A route that turns out too expensive or too tightly bound to a third party is hard to reverse once browsers, delivery code, and store publication are all built around it.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates.** No gate eliminated an option in contention. The inherited constraints below are background rather than discriminating criteria: all three routes deliver the server-assigned event revision without forking it, all three serve a browser's first board view and its recovery after an outage from the same seven-day revision retention, and none of them requires browser-side merging of offline commands.
+
+**Weighted criteria**, in descending importance:
+
+- **Update freshness** *(major)* — how long a committed revision takes to become visible in a connected browser.
+- **Recurring cost** *(major)* — the per-connection, per-message, or per-market-week spend the route imposes on the project.
+- **Operational burden** *(major)* — the connection lifecycle, capacity sizing, and failure handling the project must staff and run itself.
+- **Vendor coupling** *(major)* — how much of the delivery path depends on a third party's protocol, pricing, and uptime.
+- **Browser-side complexity** *(moderate)* — the work every browser client must carry to stay current.
+- **Store-side complexity** *(moderate)* — the work the change store must do to serve the route.
+- **Availability accountability** *(moderate)* — who is answerable for the board being up during published event hours.
+- **Data handling** *(minor)* — whether operational event data leaves project infrastructure.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** The browser issues a snapshot request every five seconds against the board's HTTPS endpoint. The server answers from the change store, using the revision sequence as a cursor so that a request whose cursor has not moved can be answered cheaply and the browser can be told explicitly that nothing changed. No long-lived connection is involved; the browser is a poll loop. This is the simplest route to operate because it introduces no new subsystem — the board already has an HTTPS command boundary and a change store, and both are reused as they are.
+
+**Demonstration.** 800 browsers polling every five seconds produce roughly 160 snapshot reads per second at steady state, sustained through every published market hour, whether or not a single edit is committed.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Update freshness — **weak.** A committed revision becomes visible to a browser only at its next poll tick, so the observed delay runs up to the full five seconds plus request and response time. The 15-second goal is met, but with the interval itself consuming a third of the budget before any network or render cost, and the only way to improve it is a shorter interval, which multiplies the read load directly.
+- Recurring cost — **strong.** No third party bills the project per connection or per message. The cost is the project's own compute for the read rate described above, which is a capacity decision rather than a line item.
+- Operational burden — **strong.** There is no connection lifecycle to operate, no keepalive, and no reconnect logic. The failure modes are the ordinary HTTP ones the project already handles.
+- Vendor coupling — **strong.** No third-party service sits in the delivery path.
+- Browser-side complexity — **partial.** The client loop is small, but each browser schedules its own repeats, and browsers throttle timers in background tabs, so the effective interval can stretch well past five seconds for a tab the coordinator has not focused — a case that then needs its own handling.
+- Store-side complexity — **partial.** The store must support cursor or sequence queries that distinguish "no new revisions" from "here is what changed," rather than serving whole snapshots repeatedly.
+- Availability accountability — **strong.** The project's own endpoints are the only dependency, and the board is down exactly when the project is down.
+- Data handling — **strong.** Operational data stays entirely within project infrastructure.
+
+**Summary.** The cheapest route to operate and read, and the slowest to show a committed change.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSocket connections
+
+**Description.** The project operates the socket tier. Each subscribed browser holds one WebSocket connection to the server; when a revision is committed for an event, the server pushes it on every socket subscribed to that event. When a connection drops, the browser reconnects and resumes from the last revision it saw, and the server serves the revisions it missed from the revision log. The delivery path gains a persistent, connection-oriented subsystem that the project runs, sizes, and keeps alive itself.
+
+**Demonstration.** A single commit on one event produces one revision push on each socket subscribed to that event, with no per-browser read. A browser disconnected for 20 minutes returns and is served the revisions it missed from the seven-day retention window.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Update freshness — **strong.** A committed revision is pushed at commit time, so the delay is bounded by commit-to-push rather than by a poll interval, and opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second are absorbed without any read amplification.
+- Recurring cost — **strong.** There is no per-connection vendor fee; the project pays for the socket capacity it provisions, which is a fixed cost that does not grow with market hours or idle browsers.
+- Operational burden — **weak.** The project owns connection capacity for 800 concurrent sockets, session lifecycle, keepalive and eviction, and the reconnect storms that follow a restart or a network outage. The socket tier must be sized for the burst and for the reconnect wave, not for the average, and failures here are of a kind the project has not operated before.
+- Vendor coupling — **strong.** No third-party realtime service sits in the delivery path, and the wire protocol is one the project controls.
+- Browser-side complexity — **partial.** The browser must implement reconnect and resumption rather than a plain timer loop, and it must reason explicitly about which revision it is showing when a connection fails, so that a stale board is not presented as a current one.
+- Store-side complexity — **partial.** The revision log must be replayable in order, and each committed revision must reach the delivery layer reliably, so a revision committed while a socket is briefly down is not lost to the connection that missed it.
+- Availability accountability — **partial.** The project's own socket tier is the only dependency, so the project is fully answerable for the board's uptime, with no third-party SLA to lean on and no second operator to call.
+- Data handling — **strong.** Operational data stays within project infrastructure.
+
+**Summary.** Push-based freshness with no vendor bill, paid for in connection and reconnect operations the project owns.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A realtime provider — PubNub, Ably, or a comparable service — is given the project's committed revisions and fans them out to browsers over its own subscription protocol and SDK. The project publishes each committed revision to the provider's channel for the event and the provider maintains the subscriptions on the project's behalf. The delivery path gains a dependency whose connection handling, reconnect behavior, and fan-out are the provider's product, and whose pricing scales with the number of concurrent subscriptions the project holds open.
+
+**Demonstration.** The project publishes one committed revision to the provider's channel for that event; the provider holds up to 800 concurrent subscriptions on the project's behalf and delivers the revision to each browser attached to them.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Update freshness — **strong.** The provider's fan-out is push-based, and the observed delay is comparable to self-hosted sockets.
+- Recurring cost — **weak.** The provider charges for concurrent connections and message volume, so the bill scales with the board's busiest shape — 800 simultaneous browsers — and is incurred across every published market week whether or not any change occurs.
+- Operational burden — **strong.** The provider operates connections, reconnection, and fan-out. The project publishes revisions, and must reconcile the case where the store committed a revision that never reached the provider.
+- Vendor coupling — **weak.** The browser's wire protocol, the delivery tier's API, and the price of scale all belong to the provider. Reversing this later means changing the browser client and the delivery tier, not just swapping a backend.
+- Browser-side complexity — **strong.** The browser uses the provider's subscription model and SDK, and much of the reconnect handling comes with it.
+- Store-side complexity — **partial.** Publication must be reconciled against the store, and a committed revision that fails to publish has to survive the gap so the store and the provider's view do not silently diverge.
+- Availability accountability — **partial.** The provider's SLA backs the fan-out, but end-to-end board availability still rides on the provider's uptime, and a provider outage is a board outage the project cannot fix directly.
+- Data handling — **partial.** Operational event data leaves project infrastructure for a third party's servers, which is acceptable under the project's data rules but no longer fully inside the project's control.
+
+**Summary.** The least operational work for the project, at the price of a recurring fee and a standing dependency on one provider.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSocket connections.**
+
+Two options deliver push-based freshness, so freshness does not separate the field: five-second polling is eliminated on cost of freshness, because a committed change would routinely wait out a poll interval that consumes a third of the 15-second budget before any network cost, and because the only remedy is a shorter interval that multiplies reads against the store. That leaves self-hosted sockets and the hosted service, which split cleanly on the remaining major criteria: the hosted service is strong on operational burden and browser-side complexity but weak on recurring cost and vendor coupling, while self-hosted sockets are strong on recurring cost and vendor coupling and weak on operational burden and browser-side complexity. With no criterion beyond those four separating them, the tie breaks on what the project has said it will own: fast updates without a realtime vendor, at the price of connection and reconnect work the project carries itself.
+
+Committing to this route obliges three things downstream. The change store must keep committed revisions replayable in order and must publish each committed revision reliably to the delivery layer, so that a revision is never lost to a connection that was briefly absent. The delivery node must own socket sessions — accepting and closing connections, authorizing subscriptions, pushing committed revisions, and bringing a reconnecting browser forward from the revision it last saw — sized for 800 concurrent browsers and for reconnect waves after a restart. The browser-sync node must implement reconnection and resumption from the last known revision, and must never let a failed connection present itself as an unchanged board. The three nodes are designed around those obligations.
+
+**Tenet closure.** No tenet decided this choice, and that is worth recording: the existing tenets constrain it — a committed edit keeps one authoritative event revision regardless of delivery route, connectivity failures stay visible, commands are authorized server-side, and self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted — but none of them prefers one route over another. The lean that actually decided it, accepting operational work in order to avoid a recurring realtime vendor fee and provider coupling, is recorded here and in the high-level design's Key Design Decisions, and is not currently stated as a tenet.
+
+**Reconsidering.** The judgment above should be re-run against the same elements if the board's concurrency grows well beyond 800 browsers, if the team cannot staff socket operations through market weekends, or if the recurring realtime fee falls to a level that no longer offsets the operational work.
+
