 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSocket connections.** The project operates the socket tier itself and pushes each committed revision to the sockets subscribed to that event, serving catch-up from the revision log when a browser reconnects. Five-second HTTPS polling and a hosted realtime service were the alternatives. Polling was rejected for its repeated reads and seconds of lag on every browser; a hosted realtime service was rejected for its recurring per-connection fees and the provider coupling it would introduce. Self-hosting costs the project the connection capacity, session management, and reconnect handling that a hosted service would have carried, and the project accepts that cost in exchange for fast updates and no dependency on a realtime vendor. The weighing behind this choice is recorded in `docs/decisions/delivery-approach.md`.
